Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042177/state.json" "file_sha256" "d94aeaff82090a7938a0d72149a5f134f48e63b50ea71550611d24627b4ff4ab"} {"question" "q-164275" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the $z$-intercepts, we plug $x=0$ and $y=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(0-2)^{2}}{10}+\\frac{(0-1)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{40} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4}{10}+\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{40} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{z}^{2}}{40} & =\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =16 \\\\ z & =\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $z=-4$ and $z=4.$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $z$-axis are $(0,0,-4)$ and $(0,0,4).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042177/state.json" "file_sha256" "d94aeaff82090a7938a0d72149a5f134f48e63b50ea71550611d24627b4ff4ab"} {"question" "q-164275" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042177/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "40c9b47f84a46075b258520c8bb344da4c894b0d0c5fd9f11b282ef313c0163f"} {"question" "q-164275" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042177/state.json" "file_sha256" "d94aeaff82090a7938a0d72149a5f134f48e63b50ea71550611d24627b4ff4ab"} {"question" "q-164275" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$(0,0,-1)$ and $(0,0,16)$"] ["text" "$(0,0,-16)$ and $(0,0,1)$"] ["text" "$(0,0,-4)$ and $(0,0,1)$"] ["text" "$(0,0,-4)$ and $(0,0,4)$"] ["text" "$(0,0,1)$ and $(0,0,16)$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042177/state.json" "file_sha256" "d94aeaff82090a7938a0d72149a5f134f48e63b50ea71550611d24627b4ff4ab"} {"question" "q-164275" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$(0,0,-4)$ and $(0,0,4)$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042177/state.json" "file_sha256" "d94aeaff82090a7938a0d72149a5f134f48e63b50ea71550611d24627b4ff4ab"} {"question" "q-73214" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the ellipsoid $\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{9}+(y+2)^{2}+\\frac{(z-2)^{2}}{9}=1$, find its intercepts with the $y$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042177/state.json" "file_sha256" "d94aeaff82090a7938a0d72149a5f134f48e63b50ea71550611d24627b4ff4ab"} {"question" "q-73214" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the $y$-intercepts, we plug $x=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(0+1)^{2}}{9}+(y+2)^{2}+\\frac{(0-2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}+(y+2)^{2}+\\frac{4}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{9}+(y+2)^{2} & =1 \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =\\frac{4}{9} \\\\ y+2 & =\\pm \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ y & =-2\\pm \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $y=-\\frac{8}{3}$ and $y=-\\frac{4}{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $y$-axis are $(0,-\\frac{8}{3},0)$ and $(0,-\\frac{4}{3},0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042177/state.json" "file_sha256" "d94aeaff82090a7938a0d72149a5f134f48e63b50ea71550611d24627b4ff4ab"} {"question" "q-73214" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042177/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "40c9b47f84a46075b258520c8bb344da4c894b0d0c5fd9f11b282ef313c0163f"} {"question" "q-73214" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042177/state.json" "file_sha256" "d94aeaff82090a7938a0d72149a5f134f48e63b50ea71550611d24627b4ff4ab"} {"question" "q-73214" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$(0,-2,0)$ and $(0,2,0)$"] ["text" "$(0,-\\frac{16}{9},0)$ only"] ["text" "$(0,-\\frac{4}{3},0)$ only"] ["text" "$(0,-\\frac{8}{3},0)$ and $(0,-\\frac{4}{3},0)$"] ["text" "$(0,0,0)$ and $(0,-\\frac{4}{3},0)$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042177/state.json" "file_sha256" "d94aeaff82090a7938a0d72149a5f134f48e63b50ea71550611d24627b4ff4ab"} {"question" "q-73214" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$(0,-\\frac{8}{3},0)$ and $(0,-\\frac{4}{3},0)$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042177/state.json" "file_sha256" "d94aeaff82090a7938a0d72149a5f134f48e63b50ea71550611d24627b4ff4ab"} {"question" "q-164311" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the sphere $(x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}+(z-2)^{2}=9,$ what is the domain of $z=z(x,y)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042177/state.json" "file_sha256" "d94aeaff82090a7938a0d72149a5f134f48e63b50ea71550611d24627b4ff4ab"} {"question" "q-164311" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the equation of the sphere, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}+(z-2)^{2} & =9 \\\\ (z-2)^{2} & =9-((x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}) \\\\ z-2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{9-((x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2})} \\\\ z & =2\\pm \\sqrt{9-((x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9-((x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2})\\ge 0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\le 9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis inequality describes the interior and boundary of the circle \n\n$$\n(x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=9\n$$\n\nwhich is a circle of radius $3$ centered at $(3,1)$ in the $xy$-plane." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042177/state.json" "file_sha256" "d94aeaff82090a7938a0d72149a5f134f48e63b50ea71550611d24627b4ff4ab"} {"question" "q-164311" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042177/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "40c9b47f84a46075b258520c8bb344da4c894b0d0c5fd9f11b282ef313c0163f"} {"question" "q-164311" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042177/state.json" "file_sha256" "d94aeaff82090a7938a0d72149a5f134f48e63b50ea71550611d24627b4ff4ab"} {"question" "q-164311" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "The interior and boundary of the circle of radius $3$ centered at $(1,-3)$"] ["text" "The interior and boundary of the circle of radius $3$ centered at $(3,1)$"] ["text" "The interior and boundary of the circle of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at $(-3,1)$"] ["text" "The interior and boundary of the circle of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at $(1,3)$"] ["text" "The interior and boundary of the circle of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at $(3,2)$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042177/state.json" "file_sha256" "d94aeaff82090a7938a0d72149a5f134f48e63b50ea71550611d24627b4ff4ab"} {"question" "q-164311" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The interior and boundary of the circle of radius $3$ centered at $(3,1)$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042177/state.json" "file_sha256" "d94aeaff82090a7938a0d72149a5f134f48e63b50ea71550611d24627b4ff4ab"} {"question" "q-164317" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the ellipsoid $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{10}+{y}^{2}+\\frac{(z+1)^{2}}{5}=1$, find the domain of $z=z(x,y).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042177/state.json" "file_sha256" "d94aeaff82090a7938a0d72149a5f134f48e63b50ea71550611d24627b4ff4ab"} {"question" "q-164317" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the ellipsoid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{10}+{y}^{2}+\\frac{(z+1)^{2}}{5} & =1 \\\\ (z+1)^{2} & =5(1-\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{10}-{y}^{2}) \\\\ z+1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{5(1-\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{10}-{y}^{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{10}-{y}^{2}\\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{10}+{y}^{2}\\le 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the interior of the ellipse $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{10}+{y}^{2}=1$ and its boundary. \n\nTherefore, the domain is \n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{10}+{y}^{2}\\le 1}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042177/state.json" "file_sha256" "d94aeaff82090a7938a0d72149a5f134f48e63b50ea71550611d24627b4ff4ab"} {"question" "q-164317" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042177/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "40c9b47f84a46075b258520c8bb344da4c894b0d0c5fd9f11b282ef313c0163f"} {"question" "q-164317" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042177/state.json" "file_sha256" "d94aeaff82090a7938a0d72149a5f134f48e63b50ea71550611d24627b4ff4ab"} {"question" "q-164317" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{10}<1}"] ["math" "{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{10}<1}"] ["math" "{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{10}<1}"] ["math" "{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{10}>1}"] ["math" "{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{10}+{y}^{2}\\le 1}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042177/state.json" "file_sha256" "d94aeaff82090a7938a0d72149a5f134f48e63b50ea71550611d24627b4ff4ab"} {"question" "q-164317" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{10}+{y}^{2}\\le 1}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042177/state.json" "file_sha256" "d94aeaff82090a7938a0d72149a5f134f48e63b50ea71550611d24627b4ff4ab"} {"question" "q-35518" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the ellipsoid $\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+(z+2)^{2}=1$, find the domain of $z=z(x,y).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042177/state.json" "file_sha256" "d94aeaff82090a7938a0d72149a5f134f48e63b50ea71550611d24627b4ff4ab"} {"question" "q-35518" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the ellipsoid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+(z+2)^{2} & =1 \\\\ (z+2)^{2} & =1-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} \\\\ z+2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}\\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}\\le 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the interior of the ellipse $\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1$ and its boundary.\n\nTherefore, the domain is \n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}\\le 1}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042177/state.json" "file_sha256" "d94aeaff82090a7938a0d72149a5f134f48e63b50ea71550611d24627b4ff4ab"} {"question" "q-35518" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042177/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "40c9b47f84a46075b258520c8bb344da4c894b0d0c5fd9f11b282ef313c0163f"} {"question" "q-35518" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042177/state.json" "file_sha256" "d94aeaff82090a7938a0d72149a5f134f48e63b50ea71550611d24627b4ff4ab"} {"question" "q-35518" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}<1}"] ["math" "{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}\\le 1}"] ["math" "{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}>1}"] ["math" "{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}\\le 1}"] ["text" "Entire $xy$-plane"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042177/state.json" "file_sha256" "d94aeaff82090a7938a0d72149a5f134f48e63b50ea71550611d24627b4ff4ab"} {"question" "q-35518" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}\\le 1}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042177/state.json" "file_sha256" "d94aeaff82090a7938a0d72149a5f134f48e63b50ea71550611d24627b4ff4ab"} {"question" "q-164378" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the sphere $(x+3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}+(z-4)^{2}=9,$ what is the domain of $z=z(x,y)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042177/state.json" "file_sha256" "d94aeaff82090a7938a0d72149a5f134f48e63b50ea71550611d24627b4ff4ab"} {"question" "q-164378" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the equation of the sphere, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}+(z-4)^{2} & =9 \\\\ (z-4)^{2} & =9-((x+3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}) \\\\ z-4 & =\\pm \\sqrt{9-({x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2})} \\\\ z & =4\\pm \\sqrt{9-((x+3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9-((x+3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2})\\ge 0 \\\\ (x+3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\le 9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis inequality describes the interior and boundary of the circle \n\n$$\n(x+3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=9\n$$\n\nwhich is a circle of radius $3$ centered at $(-3,1)$ in the $xy$-plane." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042177/state.json" "file_sha256" "d94aeaff82090a7938a0d72149a5f134f48e63b50ea71550611d24627b4ff4ab"} {"question" "q-164378" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042177/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "40c9b47f84a46075b258520c8bb344da4c894b0d0c5fd9f11b282ef313c0163f"} {"question" "q-164378" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042177/state.json" "file_sha256" "d94aeaff82090a7938a0d72149a5f134f48e63b50ea71550611d24627b4ff4ab"} {"question" "q-164378" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "The interior and boundary of the circle of radius $3$ centered at $(-3,1)$"] ["text" "The interior and boundary of the circle of radius $3$ centered at $(3,-1)$"] ["text" "The interior and boundary of the circle of radius $9$ centered at $(-3,-1)$"] ["text" "The interior and boundary of the circle of radius $9$ centered at $(3,-1)$"] ["text" "The interior and boundary of the circle of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at $(-3,4)$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042177/state.json" "file_sha256" "d94aeaff82090a7938a0d72149a5f134f48e63b50ea71550611d24627b4ff4ab"} {"question" "q-164378" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The interior and boundary of the circle of radius $3$ centered at $(-3,1)$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/ja