"q-164216" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042177/state.json" "file_sha256" "d94aeaff82090a7938a0d72149a5f134f48e63b50ea71550611d24627b4ff4ab"} {"question" "q-164216" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "An ellipse with semi-axes $2\\sqrt{2}$ and $2,$ centered at $(3,0)$ in the $xz$-plane"] ["text" "An ellipse with semi-axes $2\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\sqrt{2},$ centered at $(0,0)$ in the $xz$-plane"] ["text" "An ellipse with semi-axes $2\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\sqrt{2},$ centered at $(0,3)$ in the $xz$-plane"] ["text" "An ellipse with semi-axes $4$ and $1,$ centered at $(0,0)$ in the $xz$-plane"] ["text" "An ellipse with semi-axes $4$ and $1,$ centered at $(3,0)$ in the $xz$-plane"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042177/state.json" "file_sha256" "d94aeaff82090a7938a0d72149a5f134f48e63b50ea71550611d24627b4ff4ab"} {"question" "q-164216" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "An ellipse with semi-axes $4$ and $1,$ centered at $(3,0)$ in the $xz$-plane"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042177/state.json" "file_sha256" "d94aeaff82090a7938a0d72149a5f134f48e63b50ea71550611d24627b4ff4ab"} {"question" "e-5311" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What are the radius $r$ and the coordinates of the center $C$ for the sphere $(x+1)^2 + y^2 + z^2 + 8z = 20?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042177/example-5311.json" "file_sha256" "a7c47a4a4379f57b4ae7c7cbd67826498873802fc753a03c09489d7af95cae26"} {"question" "e-5311" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The equation of a sphere centered at $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ with radius $r > 0$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-x_0)^2 + (y-y_0)^2 + (z-z_0)^2 = r^2.\n$$\n\nBy completing the square for the variable $z$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+8z & =20 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2}+({z}^{2}+8z+16) & =20+16 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2}+(z+4)^{2} & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write our equation as\n\n$$\n(x-(-1))^2 + (y-0)^2 + (z-(-4))^2 = 6^2,\n$$\n\nwhich gives us the center at the point $C(-1,0,-4)$ and the radius $r=6.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042177/example-5311.json" "file_sha256" "a7c47a4a4379f57b4ae7c7cbd67826498873802fc753a03c09489d7af95cae26"} {"question" "e-5263" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the ellipsoid $\\dfrac {(x-2)^2} {2} +  {y^2} + \\dfrac{(z+1)^2}{2} =1,$ find its intercepts with the $x$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042177/example-5263.json" "file_sha256" "0838f18f3b7092322b1dfd1832dcb81301964d22ef0240114233cc0a3160a009"} {"question" "e-5263" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the $x$-intercepts, we plug $y=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}+(0)^{2}+\\frac{(0+1)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =1 \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =2\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =1,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $x$-axis are $\\left(1, 0, 0\\right)$ and $\\left(3, 0, 0\\right).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042177/example-5263.json" "file_sha256" "0838f18f3b7092322b1dfd1832dcb81301964d22ef0240114233cc0a3160a009"} {"question" "e-12554" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the ellipsoid $\\dfrac {x^2} {4} +  \\dfrac {y^2} 9  + z^2 = 1$, find the domain of $z=z(x,y).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042177/example-12554.json" "file_sha256" "a8635edfe89adc5be2fe27dc637cb4a5581753c85e5b6046380309fbfadbf905"} {"question" "e-12554" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the ellipsoid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {z}^{2} & =1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} \\\\ z & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}\\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}\\le 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the interior of the ellipse $\\dfrac {x^2} {4} +  \\dfrac {y^2} 9 =  1$ and its boundary.  \n\nTherefore, the domain is of $z = z(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\bigg\\{ (x, y) \\,:\\, \\dfrac {x^2} {4} +  \\dfrac {y^2} 9 \\leq  1 \\bigg\\}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042177/example-12554.json" "file_sha256" "a8635edfe89adc5be2fe27dc637cb4a5581753c85e5b6046380309fbfadbf905"} {"question" "e-5312" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the trace in the $xy$-plane of the sphere $x^2 + (y+2)^2 + (z-3)^2 = 25?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042177/example-5312.json" "file_sha256" "53bf5107aa44af9eac7133dbc303a0123ca9a245e8420d9f07715fae1bdc40fb"} {"question" "e-5312" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the trace in the $xy$-plane, we substitute $z=0$ into the equation of the sphere:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}+(0-3)^{2} & =25 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}+9 & =25 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of the circle of radius $4$ centered at $(0,-2)$ in the $xy$-plane." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042177/example-5312.json" "file_sha256" "53bf5107aa44af9eac7133dbc303a0123ca9a245e8420d9f07715fae1bdc40fb"} {"question" "q-35518" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}\\le 1}"] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042177/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "e12ee37a6be4d509727be10ec2282cc158f024bef96c85ca85ffeff4ad42e543" "reviewer_session" "01a11908-fa8d-7c61-8e03-c1185d0b9506" "rationale" "Solving gives z=-2±sqrt(1-(x-1)^2/16-y^2/4). Real z exists exactly when (x-1)^2/16+y^2/4≤1, including the boundary where the radicand is zero. This is original option d and agrees with the authentic worked solution and stored key. Saved grading marked the submitted strict-inequality option b incorrect; it does not contradict this derived answer." "bindings" {"problem" "b9d97426145a874427841aa84725961960c333379592ad597640a0c9c2bff281" "worked_solution" "0d213b38be6cc78a264f2723d32ee053cf19a01404a4b3fd9c8fea74fafae566" "original_field" "507fc7d58ae4edcce2d3f4792537f4fb5e770ece06a75456e24f0698c2c98b8b" "reviewed_field" "f3c082ce0235c34fcf9318b690b02aadf1ff1986a298e4dcb1866b7c863ce441" "raw_widget" "d800c0b0421872c76548778a55a4bc29c105b5fe70fccc26810a9bff9e5bb03b"} "evidence_files" [{"path" "q-35518-before.png" "sha256" "abee9089813e11a4b0755a6677e90001449da7564dd15ff0601744c89586fa52"} {"path" "q-35518-before.json" "sha256" "76c4acceaac764664ee32ee56f7e4d52ad161978568814697ff443836bb14a20"} {"path" "history-q-35518.png" "sha256" "26f448fa417a9fed8783f9a19122e2f991779a80ca0d67570d6ad1a3d6987d29"} {"path" "history-q-35518.json" "sha256" "656bb02782bb3729baeed720eca7ffaf5add53df26f2e7d51a78a8f4899013b7"} {"path" "q-35518-after.json" "sha256" "41d77afc1c39bd40485dfe93e0722490ed18257d24404edf826188bfe1f9d94b"} {"path" "edb-import/questions.edn" "sha256" "7bede9575473375a4f768754e69ab69898e754245b723f9a7ce9dbdbd16bd349"}]} {"question" "q-35518" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}<1}"] ["math" "{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}\\le 1}"] ["math" "{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}>1}"] ["math" "{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}\\le 1}"] ["text" "Entire $xy$-plane"]] "category" "reviewed_ma_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042177/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "e12ee37a6be4d509727be10ec2282cc158f024bef96c85ca85ffeff4ad42e543" "rationale" "Solving gives z=-2±sqrt(1-(x-1)^2/16-y^2/4). Real z exists exactly when (x-1)^2/16+y^2/4≤1, including the boundary where the radicand is zero. This is original option d and agrees with the authentic worked solution and stored key. Saved grading marked the submitted strict-inequality option b incorrect; it does not contradict this derived answer."}] "usage" {"q-73214" [] "e-5311" [] "q-164186" [] "q-35518" [] "q-70560" [] "q-70563" [] "q-70927" [] "e-5263" [] "e-12554" [] "e-5312" []} "basis" 1542 "retractions" [[17592186125129 :question/problem "What is the radius of the sphere $x^{2} + y^{2} + 6y + (z - 1)^{2} = 16$?"] [17592186125128 :question/problem "What are the radius $r$ and the coordinates of the center $C$ for the sphere $x^{2} + (y + 3)^{2} + (z - 1)^{2} = 2$?"] [17592186125133 :question/problem "Given the ellipsoid $((x + 1)^{2})/(9) + (y + 2)^{2} + ((z - 2)^{2})/(9) = 1$, find its intercepts with the $y$-axis."] [17592186124803 :question/problem "Given the ellipsoid $((x - 1)^{2})/(16) + \\frac{y^{2}}{4} + (z + 2)^{2} = 1$, find the domain of $z = z(x, y)$."] [17592186124803 :question/answer-fields 17592186121840] [17592186124742 :question/problem "Given the sphere $x^{2} + (y + 3)^{2} + (z - 1)^{2} = 2$, what is the domain of $z = z(x, y)$?"] [17592186125130 :question/problem "What is the trace in the $xz$-plane of the sphere $(x - 2)^{2} + y^{2} + (z + 2)^{2} = 16$?"] [17592186124230 :question/problem "What are the radius $r$ and the coordinates of the center $C$ for the sphere $(x+1)^2 + y^2 + z^2 + 8z = 20$?"] [17592186124230 :question/worked-solution "The equation of a sphere centered at $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ with radius $r > 0$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-x_0)^2 + (y-y_0)^2 + (z-z_0)^2 = r^2\n$$\n\nBy completing the square for the variable $z$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + 1)^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2} + 8z &= 20 \\\\\n(x + 1)^{2} + y^{2} + (z^{2} + 8z + 16) &= 20 + 16 \\\\\n(x + 1)^{2} + y^{2} + (z + 4)^{2} &= 36\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write our equation as\n\n$$\n(x-(-1))^2 + (y-0)^2 + (z-(-4))^2 = 6^2\n$$\n\nwhich gives us the center at the point $C(-1,0,-4)$ and the radius $r=6$."] [17592186124226 :question/problem "Given the ellipsoid $\\dfrac {(x-2)^2} {2} + {y^2} + \\dfrac{(z+1)^2}{2} =1$, find its intercepts with the $x$-axis."] [17592186124226 :question/worked-solution "To find the $x$-intercepts, we plug $y=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n((x - 2)^{2})/(2) + (0)^{2} + ((0 + 1)^{2})/(2) &= 1 \\\\\n((x - 2)^{2})/(2) + \\frac{1}{2} &= 1 \\\\\n((x - 2)^{2})/(2) &= \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n(x - 2)^{2} &= 1 \\\\\nx - 2 &= ± 1 \\\\\nx &= 2 ± 1 \\\\\nx &= 1, 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $x$-axis are\n\n$$\n\\left(1, 0, 0\\right)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\left(3, 0, 0\\right)\n$$"] [17592186124130 :question/problem "Given the ellipsoid $\\dfrac {x^2} {4} + \\dfrac {y^2} 9 + z^2 = 1$, find the domain of $z=z(x,y)$."] [17592186124130 :question/worked-solution "From the ellipsoid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{2}}{4} + \\frac{y^{2}}{9} + z^{2} &= 1 \\\\\nz^{2} &= 1 - \\frac{x^{2}}{4} - \\frac{y^{2}}{9} \\\\\nz &= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{x^{2}}{4} - \\frac{y^{2}}{9}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have the following:\n\n$$\n1 - \\frac{x^{2}}{4} - \\frac{y^{2}}{9} \\ge 0; \\frac{x^{2}}{4} + \\frac{y^{2}}{9} \\le 1\n$$\n\nwhich gives the interior of the ellipse\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {x^2} {4} + \\dfrac {y^2} 9 = 1\n$$\n\nand its boundary.\n\nTherefore, the domain is of $z = z(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\bigg\\{(x, y) \\,:\\, \\dfrac {x^2} {4} + \\dfrac {y^2} 9 \\leq 1 \\bigg\\}\n$$"] [17592186124231 :question/problem "What is the trace in the $xy$-plane of the sphere $x^2 + (y+2)^2 + (z-3)^2 = 25$?"] [17592186124231 :question/worked-solution "To find the trace in the $xy$-plane, we substitute $z=0$ into the equation of the sphere:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + (y + 2)^{2} + (0 - 3)^{2} &= 25 \\\\\nx^{2} + (y + 2)^{2} + 9 &= 25 \\\\\nx^{2} + (y + 2)^{2} &= 16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of the circle of radius $4$ centered at $(0,-2)$ in the $xy$-plane."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186121472 17592186121473 17592186121474 17592186121475 17592186121476 17592186121841 17592186121842 17592186121843 17592186121844 17592186121845 17592186123791 17592186123792 17592186123793 17592186123794 17592186123795 17592186123797 17592186123798 17592186123799 17592186123800 17592186123801 17592186123803 17592186123804 17592186123805 17592186123806 17592186123807 17592186123821 17592186123822 17592186123823 17592186123824 17592186123825] "immutable_fields" [[17592186121471 :answer-field/correct] [17592186121471 :answer-field/id] [17592186121471 :answer-field/key] [17592186121471 :answer-field/typ